/* data/learn-c/phy-12-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-08"] = {"8.1":{"s":"This chapter goes beyond the electric field of Class 11. It uses Gauss' law for symmetric charges. Then it covers electric potential and potential energy, conductors and dielectrics, and capacitors and their energy. It ends with the Van de Graaff generator.","f":[{"x":"phi = q/epsilon_0","n":"Gauss' law: total electric flux out of any closed surface; q = net charge inside that surface (air or vacuum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.1, p.186"}],"w":["Only the charge INSIDE the closed surface counts for the flux; charges outside change the field but not the total flux."],"x":["In Class 11 you studied Coulomb's law, the electric field and field lines. This chapter goes further. It uses Gauss's law to find fields of symmetric charge distributions, and it introduces electric potential, which describes the same electric effects with a scalar instead of a vector.","Then the chapter looks at conductors and insulators, at dielectrics, and at capacitors, which store charge and energy. It ends with the displacement current and the Van de Graaff generator.","Keep two ideas apart. The field E is a vector and tells the force on a unit charge. The potential V is a scalar and tells the energy of a unit charge. Many problems are easier with V, because scalars simply add."],"e":"Two charges of +1 µC and −1 µC: finding the field at a point needs two vectors added with directions. Finding the potential at that point is a plain sum of two numbers."},"8.2":{"s":"Gauss' law makes the field easy to find when the charge is symmetric. Outside a charged sphere the field is as if all charge sat at the centre: E = q/(4πε₀r²). Inside a charged conducting shell the field is zero. Near a large flat sheet with charge density σ, E = σ/(2ε₀), the same at every distance.","f":[{"x":"E = q/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r^2)","n":"Field of a uniformly charged spherical shell at a point outside it (r > R), as if all charge q sat at the centre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = sigma/epsilon_0","n":"Field just at the surface of the charged shell (r = R); sigma = charge per unit area.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = lambda/(2*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Field at distance r from a very long straight wire; lambda = charge per unit length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = sigma/(2*epsilon_0)","n":"Field near a large flat sheet of charge; the same at every distance, pointing away from the sheet if sigma is positive.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.188"}],"w":["Inside a uniformly charged shell E = 0, but the potential is not zero: it equals the value at the surface.","Sheet of charge: σ/(2ε₀). Just outside a charged conductor: σ/ε₀. Do not mix them up."],"x":["Gauss's law says that the total electric flux through any closed surface equals the charge inside it divided by ε₀: Φ = q_enclosed/ε₀. The law is always true, but it helps most when the charge has a symmetry, so that you can choose a surface on which the field has a constant size and is perpendicular to the surface.","Sphere (shell or conductor) of charge Q and radius R: outside, E = Q/(4πε₀r²), as if all the charge were at the centre. Inside a hollow shell, E = 0, because a surface inside encloses no charge.","Infinite line charge with linear density λ: E = λ/(2πε₀r), falling as 1/r. Infinite plane sheet with surface density σ: E = σ/(2ε₀), the same at any distance, and directed away from a positive sheet. Between the plates of a large charged capacitor, the two sheets add to give E = σ/ε₀, and outside the plates the fields cancel.","At the surface of a charged conductor, the field is perpendicular to the surface and has size σ/ε₀."],"e":"A large sheet carries σ = 8.85 × 10⁻⁶ C/m². Its field is E = σ/(2ε₀) = 8.85 × 10⁻⁶/(2 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 5 × 10⁵ N/C on either side."},"8.3":{"s":"Electric potential at a point is the work done per unit positive charge to bring it from infinity to that point, measured in volt (J/C). Potential difference between two points is the work per unit charge to move a charge between them. The field points from high to low potential, and E is the rate at which potential falls with distance.","f":[{"x":"U = q_1*q_2/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Potential energy of two point charges r apart (zero at infinite distance).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.189"},{"x":"V = U/q","n":"Potential = potential energy per unit charge; unit volt = joule per coulomb.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.190"},{"x":"E = -dV/dx","n":"The field is the negative potential gradient: it points the way the potential falls fastest.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.190"},{"x":"1 eV = 1.6*10^-19 J","n":"Electron volt: energy gained by an electron through 1 V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.189"}],"w":["Potential is a scalar (add with signs); field is a vector (add as vectors).","The minus sign in E = -dV/dx: the field points from high to low potential."],"x":["Electric potential at a point is the work done, by an outside agent, in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without speeding it up. Its unit is the volt, 1 V = 1 J/C. It is a scalar.","The potential difference between two points A and B is the work per unit charge in moving from A to B: V_B − V_A = W/q. The electrostatic force is conservative, so this work does not depend on the path taken, only on the end points.","The potential energy of a charge q at a point of potential V is U = qV. A positive charge released from rest moves from high to low potential, and its potential energy falls while its kinetic energy rises. A negative charge moves the other way.","The field and the potential are related: the field points in the direction in which the potential falls most quickly, and E = −dV/dx. In a uniform field E, between points a distance d apart along the field, the potential difference is V = Ed. So the unit of field can also be written as V/m."],"e":"An electron (charge 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) accelerated through 100 V gains an energy of 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J. That is 100 eV, and its speed becomes about 5.9 × 10⁶ m/s."},"8.4":{"s":"For a point charge, V = q/(4πε₀r). Potential is a scalar, so for many charges you simply add the potentials, with signs. For a dipole, V = p cos θ/(4πε₀r²): it falls faster with distance than for a single charge and is zero on the equatorial line.","f":[{"x":"V = q/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Potential at distance r from a point charge q (zero at infinity); positive for +q, negative for -q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.191"},{"x":"V = p*cos(theta)/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r^2)","n":"Potential of a short dipole (r much larger than its length); theta measured from the dipole axis, p = q*2l.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.193"},{"x":"V = (1/(4*pi*epsilon_0))*(q_1/r_1 + q_2/r_2 + ... + q_n/r_n)","n":"Superposition: potentials of several charges simply add, with their signs.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.193"}],"w":["On the equatorial line of a dipole V = 0, but E is not zero there.","A dipole's potential falls as 1/r²; a single charge's falls as 1/r."],"x":["For a point charge q, the potential at distance r is V = q/(4πε₀r) = kq/r, taking the potential to be zero at infinity. It is positive for a positive charge and negative for a negative one, and falls off as 1/r (more slowly than the field, which falls as 1/r²).","Because potential is a scalar, the potential of several point charges is the algebraic sum: V = kq₁/r₁ + kq₂/r₂ + .... Signs matter, but no directions are needed.","For an electric dipole (charges +q and −q a distance 2a apart), the potential on the axis at a distance r from the centre is V = kp/(r² − a²), roughly kp/r² when r is much larger than a, and on the perpendicular bisector it is zero. The potential of a dipole falls as 1/r², and its field as 1/r³.","The potential of a charged conducting sphere of radius R is kQ/R at the surface and the same all through the inside, because the field inside is zero."],"e":"At 0.1 m from a 5 nC charge, V = 9 × 10⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁹/0.1 = 450 V."},"8.5":{"s":"An equipotential surface has the same potential everywhere on it. No work is needed to move a charge along it. Field lines always cross equipotential surfaces at right angles. For a point charge they are spheres centred on the charge; for a uniform field they are parallel planes.","w":["Two equipotential surfaces never cross: that would give two field directions at one point.","No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential surface, however long the path."],"x":["An equipotential surface is a surface on which the potential has the same value everywhere. For a point charge, the equipotential surfaces are concentric spheres around the charge. In a uniform field they are flat planes at right angles to the field.","No work is done in moving a charge from one point of an equipotential surface to another, since the potential difference is zero. This means that the electric field has no component along the surface: the field is always perpendicular to an equipotential surface.","The field points from higher to lower potential. Where the equipotential surfaces are close together, the potential changes fast, so the field is strong. Two different equipotential surfaces never cross, because a point cannot have two potentials.","The surface of a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is an equipotential, and so is the whole body of the conductor."],"e":"For a point charge, the surfaces at 450 V, 300 V and 225 V are spheres whose radii are in the ratio 1 : 1.5 : 2 (since V = kq/r), and they get farther apart as you go outwards."},"8.6":{"s":"The potential energy of two point charges is U = q₁q₂/(4πε₀r): positive for like charges, negative for unlike ones. A dipole in a uniform field has U = −pE cos θ. It is lowest (stable) when the dipole lines up with the field.","f":[{"x":"U = q_1*q_2/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r_12)","n":"Energy stored in assembling two point charges from infinity; for many charges add this over every pair.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.196"},{"x":"PE = q*V(r)","n":"Potential energy of a charge q placed where the external potential is V(r).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.197"},{"x":"tau = p*E*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a dipole in a uniform field E; theta = angle between p and E.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.198"},{"x":"U(theta) = -p*E*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy of a dipole in a uniform field (zero chosen at theta = 90 degrees).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.199"}],"w":["Put the signs of the charges into U = q₁q₂/(4πε₀r): a negative U means the pair attracts.","Dipole: torque is largest at 90 degrees; energy is lowest at 0 degrees (stable) and highest at 180 degrees (unstable)."],"x":["Bringing charges together needs work, and this work is stored as electrostatic potential energy. For two point charges q₁ and q₂ a distance r apart, U = kq₁q₂/r. It is positive for like charges (work was done against repulsion) and negative for unlike charges (the charges attract, and they would do work if allowed to come together).","For a system of three or more charges, add the energy of every pair once. For three charges, U = k(q₁q₂/r₁₂ + q₂q₃/r₂₃ + q₁q₃/r₁₃).","A dipole in a uniform field E has a potential energy U = −pE cosθ. It is lowest (−pE) when the dipole points along the field (θ = 0), which is the stable position. It is highest (+pE) when the dipole points against the field. The torque on the dipole is pE sinθ, and the net force is zero.","The work done to turn a dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ is W = pE(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂)."],"e":"Two charges of +2 µC and +3 µC, 0.3 m apart, have U = 9 × 10⁹ × 6 × 10⁻¹²/0.3 = 0.18 J."},"8.7":{"s":"Conductors have free electrons that can move through the material; insulators do not. In a conductor at rest, the field inside is zero, any extra charge sits on the outer surface, and the surface is an equipotential. In insulators, charges stay bound to their atoms.","w":["Extra charge on a conductor sits on its outer surface; the field inside the conducting material is zero even when the conductor is charged."],"x":["A conductor, such as a metal, has free electrons that can move through the whole material. An insulator, such as glass or plastic, has almost no free charges. Charges placed on an insulator stay where they are put.","In electrostatic equilibrium, a conductor has these properties. The electric field inside is zero. Any excess charge sits only on the outer surface. The field at the surface is perpendicular to it, with size σ/ε₀. The whole conductor, including its surface, is at one potential. The charge gathers more on sharp points (where the radius of curvature is small), so the field is strongest there, which explains why lightning conductors are pointed and why points leak charge.","Electrostatic shielding: the field inside a hollow conductor is zero, whatever the charges outside. A car body or a metal cage protects what is inside from outside electric fields, which is why you are safer inside a car during a thunderstorm. Sensitive instruments are put in metal boxes for the same reason."],"e":"A charged metal sphere of radius 10 cm and another of radius 5 cm are joined by a wire. They reach the same potential, so the smaller sphere carries half the charge of the larger, and a stronger surface charge density."},"8.8":{"s":"A dielectric is an insulator that can be polarised. In a field, its positive and negative charges shift slightly, so the molecules act as tiny dipoles. Polar molecules already have dipoles, which line up; non-polar ones get induced dipoles. This reduces the field inside the material by a factor k, the dielectric constant.","f":[{"x":"P = chi_e*E","n":"Polarisation (dipole moment per unit volume) of a linear dielectric; chi_e = electric susceptibility (0 for vacuum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.8, p.202"}],"w":["A dielectric lowers the field inside it by the factor k; it does not change the free charge on the plates of an isolated capacitor."],"x":["A dielectric is an insulator in which charges can shift slightly but cannot move freely. When it is put in an electric field, the positive and negative charges of its atoms are pulled in opposite directions. The material becomes polarised: one face carries a net negative charge and the opposite face a net positive charge.","There are two kinds of dielectric. In polar ones (such as water) the molecules already have a permanent dipole moment, and the field turns them to line up with it. In non-polar ones (such as nitrogen or wax), the field creates a dipole moment in each atom.","The induced surface charges set up a field inside the dielectric opposite to the applied field, so the net field is smaller. The dielectric constant K is the factor by which the field is reduced: E = E₀/K. It is also the ratio C/C₀ of the capacitances with and without the material.","Typical values: air about 1.0006, paper 3–4, glass 5–10, water 80. The large K of water is one reason why salt dissolves in it: the attraction between the ions is cut by a factor of 80."],"e":"A capacitor of 10 µF with air between its plates has 50 µF after a dielectric of K = 5 fills the gap."},"8.9":{"s":"A capacitor stores charge and energy. Its capacitance C is the charge it holds per unit potential difference: C = Q/V, in farads. In a series combination every capacitor carries the same charge and the voltages add, so the reciprocals of the capacitances add, and the result is smaller than the smallest one. In a parallel combination every capacitor has the same voltage and the charges add, so the capacitances add.","f":[{"x":"C = Q/V","n":"Capacitance; unit farad (1 F = 1 coulomb per volt). 1 microfarad = 10^-6 F, 1 picofarad = 10^-12 F.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.203"},{"x":"1/C_eq = 1/C_1 + 1/C_2 + ... + 1/C_n","n":"Capacitors in series.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"},{"x":"C_eq = C_1 + C_2 + ... + C_n","n":"Capacitors in parallel.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"},{"x":"C_eq = C/n (series), C_eq = n*C (parallel)","n":"n equal capacitors of capacitance C each.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"}],"w":["In a series combination the capacitor with the smallest capacitance has the largest voltage across it.","Series: the reciprocals add; parallel: the capacitances add. It is the opposite of resistors."],"x":["A capacitor is a device that stores charge and energy. In its simplest form it is two conductors (plates) separated by an insulator. If a potential difference V is put across it, the plates hold charges +Q and −Q, and Q = CV. The constant C is the capacitance, in farads (F = C/V). Practical capacitors are in microfarads, nanofarads or picofarads.","Series: capacitors joined end to end all carry the same charge, and the voltages add, so 1/C = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + .... The result is smaller than the smallest capacitor. Parallel: capacitors across the same voltage hold charges that add, so C = C₁ + C₂ + .... The result is larger than the largest.","A quick check: for two capacitors in series, C = C₁C₂/(C₁ + C₂). For two equal capacitors C in series, the total is C/2, and in parallel it is 2C. Compare this with resistors: the rules for capacitors are the opposite of those for resistors.","In a series combination the smaller capacitor takes the larger share of the voltage, since V = Q/C."],"e":"6 µF and 3 µF in series: C = 18/9 = 2 µF. In parallel: C = 9 µF. Across a 12 V supply in series, the charge on each is 2 µF × 12 V = 24 µC."},"8.10":{"s":"A parallel plate capacitor with plate area A and gap d has capacitance C = ε₀A/d. Filling the gap with a dielectric of constant k multiplies the capacitance by k. A slab that fills only part of the gap increases C by a smaller amount.","f":[{"x":"E = sigma/epsilon_0","n":"Field between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor (zero outside); sigma = Q/A.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.206"},{"x":"C = epsilon_0*A/d","n":"Parallel plate capacitor with air or vacuum between plates of area A, distance d apart.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.207"},{"x":"C = epsilon_0*A/(d - t + t/k)","n":"With a dielectric slab of thickness t (less than d) and dielectric constant k between the plates.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.208"},{"x":"C = k*C_0","n":"Dielectric filling the whole gap: capacitance rises k times.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.208"}],"w":["Use A in square metres and d in metres; 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².","A metal slab (k very large) of thickness t gives C = ε₀A/(d − t)."],"x":["In a parallel plate capacitor two flat plates of area A face each other, a small distance d apart. With vacuum or air between them, the capacitance is C = ε₀A/d. A larger area stores more charge for the same voltage. A smaller gap brings the plates closer, so the opposite charges hold each other and more charge can be stored.","Inside, the field is uniform: E = σ/ε₀ = Q/(ε₀A) = V/d. With a dielectric of constant K filling the gap, C = Kε₀A/d, K times larger.","What happens when you change something depends on whether the battery stays connected. With the battery connected, V stays fixed. With a dielectric inserted, C rises K times, so Q = CV also rises K times, and the energy rises. With the battery disconnected, Q stays fixed. With a dielectric inserted, C rises K times, so V = Q/C falls K times, and the energy falls K times (the dielectric is pulled in).","If the plates are pulled apart with the battery disconnected, C falls, V rises, and you do work. A thick metal slab of thickness t inserted between the plates reduces the effective gap to d − t."],"e":"Plates of area 0.02 m² separated by 1 mm: C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.02/0.001 = 177 pF."},"8.11":{"s":"No electrons cross the gap of a charging capacitor, yet the current in the circuit continues. Maxwell explained this with displacement current: a changing electric field in the gap acts like a current and also produces a magnetic field. It equals ε₀ times the rate of change of electric flux.","f":[{"x":"i_d = epsilon_0*A*(dE/dt)","n":"Displacement current between the plates (vacuum or air); it equals the conduction current i_c in the wires.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.11, p.209"}],"w":["Displacement current flows only while E is changing; for a steady field it is zero."],"x":["When a capacitor is charged, a current flows in the wires, but no charge crosses the gap between the plates. Yet the current in the wire is continuous. Maxwell saw that Ampere's law would be wrong at the gap unless something also acts in the gap.","While the capacitor charges, the electric field between the plates changes with time. Maxwell showed that a changing electric flux acts like a current, called the displacement current: I_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt. It produces a magnetic field just like a real current of the same size.","In the charging capacitor, the displacement current in the gap is equal to the conduction current in the wires, so the total current is continuous. This idea led Maxwell to predict electromagnetic waves, and then light was understood as one of them.","In a good conductor the conduction current is the main one. In an insulator, or in empty space with a changing field, only the displacement current remains."],"e":"A capacitor charged with a steady current of 2 mA has a displacement current of 2 mA in the gap too, so the magnetic field around the gap is the same as around the wire."},"8.12":{"s":"Charging a capacitor needs work, which is stored as electric energy in the field between the plates. Energy stored U = Q²/(2C) = ½ C V² = ½ Q V.","f":[{"x":"U = Q^2/(2*C) = (1/2)*C*V^2 = (1/2)*Q*V","n":"Energy stored in a capacitor with charge Q at potential difference V; Q = C*V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.12, p.210"}],"w":["Battery still connected: V stays fixed. Battery removed: Q stays fixed. Choose the form of U that keeps the fixed quantity.","While charging, the battery gives QV but only QV/2 is stored; the rest is lost as heat in the wires."],"x":["Charging a capacitor takes work, because every bit of charge added must be pushed against the field of the charge already there. This work is stored as energy in the electric field between the plates.","The potential difference rises from 0 to V as the charge rises from 0 to Q, so the average voltage is V/2 and the work is W = Q × V/2. The stored energy is therefore U = ½QV = ½CV² = Q²/(2C). Notice the factor one-half. It appears because the voltage was not constant during the charging.","The energy can also be written per unit volume: u = ½ε₀E² in vacuum (or ½Kε₀E² in a dielectric). It tells you that the energy is spread through the space where the field exists.","When a charged capacitor is connected to an uncharged one, charge flows until the voltages are equal, and some energy is lost as heat and radiation in the connecting wires, even if the wires have no resistance."],"e":"A 10 µF capacitor at 200 V stores U = ½ × 10 × 10⁻⁶ × 200² = 0.2 J."},"8.13":{"s":"The Van de Graaff generator builds up a very high voltage, of the order of millions of volts. A moving insulating belt carries charge to a large hollow metal sphere. Because charge on a conductor moves to its outer surface, charge keeps collecting there. It is used to accelerate charged particles for nuclear physics.","w":["It works because charge given to a hollow conductor moves to its outer surface, so charge keeps collecting on the dome."],"x":["The Van de Graaff generator builds up a very high voltage, millions of volts, to accelerate charged particles in physics experiments. It is based on the fact that charge placed inside a hollow conductor moves to its outer surface completely.","A large hollow metal sphere sits on an insulating column. A belt of insulating material runs round two pulleys. At the bottom, a spray of charge from a sharp comb (point discharge) is put on the belt, which carries it up into the sphere. A second comb inside the sphere collects the charge and passes it to the outer surface. Since the field inside is zero, the belt can keep bringing in more charge against the potential of the sphere.","The potential grows until the air around the sphere starts to break down (about 3 × 10⁶ V/m for dry air). A larger sphere reaches a higher potential before this happens. To go higher, the whole machine is enclosed in a steel tank filled with pressurised gas.","The high voltage is used to accelerate protons, deuterons and other ions to high energy, for studies of the nucleus."],"e":"A sphere of radius 0.5 m can reach about V = E_max × R = 3 × 10⁶ × 0.5 = 1.5 MV in dry air before sparking."}};
